<% content_for :title, "Dark mode — Shadwire" %>
<% content_for :description, "Class-based dark mode in Shadwire: the custom Tailwind variant, the Stimulus toggle, and the anti-flash script that runs before the first paint." %>
<% content_for :head do %>
  <%= tag.style rouge_stylesheet %>
<% end %>

<article class="space-y-12">
  <%= render "docs/header",
        title: "Dark mode",
        description: "A single class on <html> switches the whole site to dark mode, and the components never need to know." %>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <p class="leading-7">
      <%= docs_code("shadwire.css") %> ties Tailwind's dark variant to a class:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:variant], language: :css) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The <%= docs_code("dark") %> variant applies when an ancestor has the
      <%= docs_code(".dark") %> class, so you switch themes by adding or removing that class on
      <%= docs_code("<html>") %>. The components only use tokens, and every token has both a
      light and a dark value, so dark mode needs no code in the components.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("The toggle") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      This five-line Stimulus controller is what switches the theme on this site:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:controller], language: :javascript) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      And the button. The dark variant decides which of its two icons is shown:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:toggle_markup]) %>

    <p class="text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= docs_code("dark:hidden") %> and <%= docs_code("hidden dark:block") %> choose which
      element is visible, not its colour. That is a fine use of the <%= docs_code("dark:") %>
      prefix; using it for colours is not, as explained below.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Avoiding the flash") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      If the class is only added after the app's JavaScript loads, people who chose the dark
      theme see a white flash on every page load. To avoid it, put an inline script in
      <%= docs_code("<head>") %>, before any stylesheet, so it runs before the page is drawn:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:head_script], language: :html) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      It checks the saved preference first and only then falls back to the system setting.
      That way someone can pick the light theme on a system set to dark, and the choice is
      remembered.
    </p>

    <%= render layout: "docs/callout", locals: { icon: "info", title: "Content Security Policy" } do %>
      An app with a strict CSP has to allow this script, either through Rails' nonce
      (<code>nonce: true</code> on <code>javascript_tag</code>) or by moving it into a file
      loaded in <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> ahead of the stylesheets.
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Don't use dark: for colours") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      A hand-written dark colour repeats a decision the tokens already make, and it stops
      matching the theme as soon as someone changes the tokens:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:wrong]) %>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:right]) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      If you need a colour the tokens don't cover,
      <%= link_to "add a token", docs_theming_path, class: "font-medium underline underline-offset-4" %>.
      You give it a light and a dark value, and it keeps working when the theme changes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Testing both modes") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Check your screens in both modes. When something looks wrong in dark mode, the colour
      value itself is rarely the problem. More often two utilities with the same specificity
      are competing, or one of a pair of utilities is missing, so the class list looks right
      while the compiled CSS is not. Look at the generated CSS.
    </p>
  </section>
</article>
